<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sidney Holland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Sidney Holland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sidney Holland was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> from 1949 to 1957, leading the First National Government. His government abolished the Legislative Council with effect from 1951, broke the 1951 waterfront dispute with emergency regulations and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzus">ANZUS</a> Treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Ill health forced his retirement in 1957.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sidney-holland.jpg" alt="Sidney Holland" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gordon Burt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sidney_George_Holland_(1953)_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Holland was born on 18 October 1893 at Greendale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, the youngest of eight children of Henry Holland, a farmer and merchant who was Mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> and later a Reform Party MP. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he served with the New Zealand Field Artillery and saw action at the Battle of Messines before being invalided home with a severe illness that cost him a lung. With a brother he founded the Midland Engineering Company in Christchurch, which made horticultural spray pumps and shared its profits with its employees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1925 to 1935 he was his father's unofficial secretary and campaign manager, and in 1935 he took his father's seat of Christchurch North despite Labour's landslide. In 1940 he replaced Adam Hamilton as leader of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, and in 1941 he drew rival conservative groups into it. He was deputy chairman of the War Cabinet of 1942, which broke up that September over a miners' strike in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a>. In opposition he attacked Labour's socialism and the power of the unions, while bringing National to accept the main elements of the welfare state, and he won the election of 30 November 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>His government, in which he was also Minister of Finance, ended butter and petrol rationing and other state controls and reinstated the death penalty; eight executions followed. In the waterfront dispute of 1951 it imposed emergency regulations that sharply curtailed civil liberties, used the armed forces to unload ships and deregistered the Waterside Workers' Union, which gave in after 151 days. Holland then called a snap election and won a larger majority. The historian Redmer Yska holds that he exploited anti-Communist feeling during the dispute.</p><p>In September 1951 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzus">ANZUS</a> agreement, and troops went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> and, from 1955, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a>. National won a third term in 1954 although Labour polled more votes. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> in 1956 Holland suffered a mild heart attack or stroke at his desk, and on 12 August 1957 he announced his retirement; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keith-holyoake">Keith Holyoake</a> succeeded him on 20 September. He was knighted and died in Wellington Hospital in 1961.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sidney Holland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1950 the National government of <b>Sidney Holland</b> abolished the appointed upper house, the Legislative Council, with effect from 1 January 1951, leaving a single-chamber House of Representatives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Greendale, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-43.58,172.08&amp;year=1937" aria-label="Greendale on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8688 3709 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sidney Holland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9138 3979h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9138 3979h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-43.58,172.08&amp;year=1937">What was here in 1937? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
